WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.240
Imagine a calculation gives seven and a half people

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:03.930
in a queue.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.410
A headcount has to be a whole number.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:08.790
That tells you to look back at your reasoning.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.660
Start problems by asking what a sensible answer

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:12.490
could look like.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.830
Then use that idea to check the result.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:17.930
A count of buses is a whole number.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.810
A probability is between zero and one,

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.050
including both ends.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.250
An area has square units.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.600
Three metres along each side of a square gives

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:28.830
nine square metres.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.170
Identify the kind of answer first.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:33.890
The model also helps you predict its size.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.530
One hundred and thirty students are going on a trip.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:39.990
Each bus has seats for forty students.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.820
What is the smallest number of buses that can take

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:43.630
the whole group?

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:46.030
Make a quick estimate before you continue.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.310
Think about what happens if a bus is only partly full.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.190
Group the students into forties.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:56.630
One bus takes forty.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.610
A second takes another forty.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.150
A third brings the total on board to one

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:01.490
hundred and twenty.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.510
There are still ten students waiting.

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:07.400
Write one hundred and thirty as three lots of

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.570
forty plus ten.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.210
Those ten students still need seats.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:12.930
Put them in a fourth bus.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:16.370
Ten of its forty seats are used, so thirty are spare.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:20.250
Everyone now has a seat, and the answer is four buses.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.250
Check both sides of the boundary.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.210
Four buses make one hundred and sixty seats,

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.510
enough for all one hundred and thirty students.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.730
Three buses make one hundred and twenty seats,

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.050
so ten students would be left out.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.750
Any smaller number has even fewer seats.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.410
Four is the smallest number that works.

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:41.620
Keep the same trip.

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:45.110
This time ask how many buses are completely full if the

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:47.780
driver fills each bus before opening the next.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.740
Each of the first three has forty students.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:52.180
The fourth has ten.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:53.700
So three buses are full.

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:56.330
The last sentence of the question tells you which

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:57.140
count to give.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:00.470
Predict what a sensible answer should look like.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.110
Check every condition, then reread the question.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:04.990
Here is your notebook challenge.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.730
Pack one hundred eggs into boxes of twelve.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.230
Fill each box before opening the next.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.620
How many boxes are needed, and how many eggs go in

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:14.350
the last box?

